{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/8813-admission-counselor": {
      "title": "Admission Counselor",
      "description": "Admission Counselor | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Menu Menu and Search Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Home Human Resources Admission Counselor Admission Counselor Department Admission Overview The Admission Counselor plays a significant role in supporting the mission and enrollment goals of Ursinus College, as well as maintaining the academic profile and fiscal health of the College. As a crucial member of the Office of Admission, the Admission Counselor acts as a significant support for the Director of Admission, and other senior members of the Admission team by strategically implementing a highly efficient, team-based enrollment management model. Utilizing best practices, sound professional judgment, and strategy development, the Admission Counselor will lead recruitment efforts in an assigned recruitment territory and fully invest in meeting the established enrollment goals of the College. The Admission Counselor acts as a role model of exemplary admission practices and takes an active role in the campus community. A proactive and nimble approach to evolving environments in application development, project management, and higher education trends is needed for successful outcomes. Responsibilities Through collaboration with other departmental staff, plan and formulate strategies to attract qualified and interested students to Ursinus College Meet established enrollment goals for an assigned, targeted recruitment territory Manage all activities related to prospective students, applicants, school districts, and colleges from a specific territory/population Respond to inquiries regarding applications, admission policies and procedures, financial aid availability and program offerings at Ursinus College through prompt response to phone calls, text messages and email inquires Develop and maintain relationships with prospective students and their families through frequent telephone, email, written, and in-person contact Establish relationships with secondary school counselors and other school administrators when appropriate, through attendance at various recruitment events, as well as continual outreach throughout the admission cycle Serve as the primary liaison between prospective students/parents and College programs, curriculum, and various campus opportunities by conducting interviews and information sessions, as well as through other modes of communication Exercise professional judgment, follow established guidelines and expectations and consult with senior staff members when necessary, in reviewing applications and recommending applicants for admissions Collaborate with Student Financial Services, as well as other campus offices, to provide information necessary for conversion and yield of prospective students Participation in extensive travel within the assigned recruitment territory, in new growth markets, and in other areas as needed, which includes: school visits; college fairs (both in and out of state); specialty recruitment tours; and attendance at special programs hosted by Community-Based Organizations and Partner Program Travel can range from 8-12 weeks annually Under supervision and with guidance from the Director of Admission, coordinate outreach to Community-Based Organizations to explore and develop partnerships with external organizations to expand the reach of Ursinus College Represent Ursinus College at regional and national college fairs Extremely frequent evening and weekend work required Requirements and Qualifications Bachelor’s Degree required, with an appreciation for a liberal arts education experience 1-3 years’ experience in higher education admissions and/or recruitment, which can be as a student employee/ambassador/tour guide in an office of admission Demonstrate the highest professional demeanor and ethical behavior while representing the institution The ability to articulate the value and benefits of a liberal arts education to a variety of audiences is essential Admission staff members are expected to remain current in external market conditions and identify best admission practices Possess a celebration of diversity, both in the workplace and in student population Ability to work independently as well as establish and maintain collegial relations with other College staff, faculty, and students Ability to work collaboratively as a member of a close and integrated team of admission professionals Excellent written and oral communication skills A collaborative and transparent approach to problem-solving Ability to plan, organize and implement programs and projects related to the College’s enrollment goals A desire to join and foster a dynamic, transformative campus culture A valid driver’s license, US passport, and the ability to participate in significant travel Ability to stand in one place for significant amounts of time and ability to lift boxes up to 40 pounds Preferred Skills and Competencies Knowledge of Slate CRM Extensive knowledge of a College environment and undergraduate admission support Ability to positively interact and establish personal contacts with diverse group of students, parents, school counselors, and college personnel An in-depth knowledge of, and passion for, the liberal arts A high proficiency in multi-tasking and strategic thinking Application Interested candidates should apply here . Once you create an applicant account, you will be prompted to upload your cover letter, resume, and a list with contact information for three references to the applicant database. Ursinus is an EEO employer. Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege ursinuscollege Down goes Hopkins! 🐻🏈 @ursinus_football earns a memorable 35-14 BearFest victory to improve to 6-0 on the season! 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege The BearFest fun rolls on 🕺 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege Lots are packed, energy is high, BearFest is here 🎉 2 days ago Share End of post. Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved. Request Information Get information about Ursinus College"
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/11445-assistant-director-for-inclusion-and-belonging": {
      "title": "Assistant Director for Inclusion and Belonging",
      "description": "Assistant Director for Inclusion and Belonging | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Menu Menu and Search Home Human Resources Assistant Director for Inclusion and Belonging Assistant Director for Inclusion and Belonging Department Student Life Overview The Assistant Director for Inclusion and Belonging is a full-time professional staff member. This position assists in the shared management of the Institute for Inclusion and Equity (IIE) and the Office of Student Engagement, with specific focus on programming, education, retention, case management, and on-call duties. This position also acts as a significant support for the Director in the development and implementation of strategies to improve the overall climate around issues of difference for students at the institution. Responsibilities Develop and implement creative, positive, educational, and social programs for the student body to promote inclusion and enhance community, and a sense of belonging. Provide logistical and administrative support to the Director, including the coordination of the day-to-day operations of the IIE space. Work collaboratively alongside the Director, and Assistant Director for Student Engagement to plan and execute large-scale, campus wide programming to promote student engagement, success, and retention. Assist in the hiring, training, and supervision of the Student Programming Board. Support and provide resources for BIPOC, LGBTQ+ students, and other underrepresented student populations. Serve as the primary liaison and resource to multicultural student organizations on campus. Such support should include training, leadership development, and retreats as deemed necessary. Support religiously-affiliated and spiritually-engaged students by serving as a key resource and connector for students of all faith traditions, spiritual practices, and secular worldviews. This includes support and advising for religious student clubs and organizations. Oversee the planning and implementation of the W. R. Crigler Program for Student Success. Promote an environment that affirms inclusion, intercultural interaction, and community building activities. Assist in managing marketing strategies for the office, including consistent updates to the website, maintaining social media accounts, and promoting events and programs. Create and maintain campus partnerships to determine programmatic needs that support inclusion and equity issues on campus. Assess current programs and populations regarding inclusivity issues and use those assessments to propose and develop new initiatives with a particular focus on belonging, agency, and retention. Enhance educational initiatives and cross-cultural training during New Student Orientation, Student Government Association (SGA) Training and Resident Assistant training, etc. Work collaboratively with campus partners in planning, implementing, and evaluating Orientation, Bear Beginnings, Family Weekend, and Homecoming. Serve as a case manager and mentor for students with social and cultural challenges as it relates to belonging at Ursinus. Serve as a decision-maker for low-level student conduct cases. Serve on campus wide committees as needed. Participation in events that are scheduled for evenings and/or weekends. Serve as a member of the campus Crisis Team on an on-call rotation, responding to emergencies and critical incidents. OTHER DUTIES: The above statements are intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by the individual(s) assigned to this position. They are not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of responsibilities, duties, and skills required of personnel so classified. Job responsibilities and activities may be modified or changed at any time with or without notice. Job descriptions will be audited periodically by the Ursinus Human Resources department to ensure they are up-to-date and reflect the essential responsibilities and duties of the job. Requirements and Qualifications QUALIFICATIONS: Master’s Degree in related field preferred At least (1) year of professional experience in student affairs, which may include work experience during graduate school. Must be willing and able to reside in provided campus housing as a live-on staff member High level understanding of and experience with racial identity, socio-economic identity, religious identity, gender identity, sexual identity, gender expression, ability related identities and other underrepresented student populations Experience mentoring, advising, and providing support for multicultural students and student organizations An understanding of the residential liberal education setting Excellent interpersonal and communication skills Working knowledge of Microsoft Office applications Ability to work effectively in a team environment. Demonstrated organizational skills and ability to multi-task Many weekend and evening hours are required with this position. Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/11570-assistant-director-of-admission-transfer-outreach": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Admission, Transfer Outreach and Recruitment",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Admission, Transfer Outreach and Recruitment | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Menu Menu and Search Home Human Resources Assistant Director of Admission, Transfer Outreach and Recruitment Assistant Director of Admission, Transfer Outreach and Recruitment Department Enrollment Management Overview Responsible for the development and implementation of the College’s recruitment plan for transfer student populations. Plan and implement best practices, relationship building strategies, marketing initiatives, and key programming efforts designed to meet the College’s transfer enrollment goals. Work collaboratively with all College staff involved in the process of recruitment, advising, and enrolling transfer students. Focus on lead generation and relationship building with community and two-year college partners and organizations that support transfer initiatives. Expert in credit evaluations. Responsibilities Promote the mission and values of the college to achieve the College’s Strategic Plan. Develop and maintain a fall and spring travel schedule. This includes travel within the local community and overnight travel several hours distance away. Evening and weekends hours during peak travel times are required. These areas could be rural, suburban, and urban in nature. Travel visits to high schools and community colleges during the day along with major fairs during the day and at night. As part of the fall and spring travel, counselors are expected to remain in contact with Office of Admission on a daily basis. Such contact includes, but is not limited to, telephone contact, return of visit cards, travel evaluations, and submission of travel expenses. Develop and implement a comprehensive transfer recruitment plan; detailing communication and engagement strategies designed to increase enrollment of transfer students. Enrollment goals will not be attained at the expense of institutional quality. Identify and build relationships with community and two-year college partners and organizations serving transfer students across all recruitment territories. Master credit evaluation process as part of application review and pre-read opportunities. Responsible for understanding and accurately communicating to students the entrance requirements of the College, the academic requirements of the College or for appropriately referring student/parent questions to the appropriate College officials. Serve as a point of contact for the transfer credit re-evaluation and pre-approval process. Provide input for best practices in marketing to transfer student populations; participate in the development of publications, webpages, and digital communications designed to reach and engage transfer students. Expected to be and remain knowledgeable about newly enrolled student financial aid information. Such responsibilities include: Counseling newly enrolled students on the financial aid application procedures, financial aid eligibility and determination, and financial aid award letters. Reviewing new students’ transcripts to determine eligibility for Admissions scholarships and grants. Having a working knowledge of the financial aid calculator on website in order to educate students and families about the cost of a Ursinus degree as compared to other universities. Seeking assistance from the Office of Financial Assistance as needed for timely response to prospective students and applicants. Perform individual review of transfer student applications to make an admission decision and determine merit aid within five business days of receiving a completed student file. Responsible for record maintenance of each student. This includes follow-up for collecting missing credentials, updating or correcting student data within the CRM. (Slate) Support the development of effective articulation agreements and transfer guides with community and two year colleges by maintaining accurate course equivalencies. Evaluate programs and services for transfer students through surveys of admitted and enrolled transfer students and community college advisors. Expected to be involved in inter-departmental committees within the College and external professional organizations and activities as deemed appropriate and approved by the Director of Admission. Represent the College at on- and off-campus recruitment events during work hours, in the evenings, and on the weekends. Develop counselor newsletter and communication sequence. Maintain a small first year student population with strategic fall and spring travel along with application review and yielding applicants to meet the territory goals. Duties are subject to change based on the needs of the Office. As agents of the College, counselors are required to represent the College as trained professionals. Professional dress and demeanor are essential. As a visible agent of Ursinus College, highest standards of conduct, attitude, confidentiality and appearance are expected in all aspects of performance. Understand and adhere to all applicable safety policies and procedures including safety education, accident preventions, and safe work environment. All other duties as assigned. Requirements and Qualifications Bachelor’s degree required. Master’s degree preferred. 2-4 years of direct experience in recruitment/admissions is required. 1-2 years of experience with transfer population or working with or in a community college setting is preferred. Ability to partner with College staff, administrators, students and partners as an essential function Ability to communicate effectively both orally and in writing with a diverse population Ability to maintain and manage information and documentation accurately Ability to travel extensively year round Understanding of technology with preferred experience in Slate or comparable CRM Must be goal oriented, self-motivated, and flexible Must have strong interpersonal skills, be computer literate and have a valid driver’s license and a personal vehicle Understanding of and ability to articulate and support the mission of Ursinus College Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/11549-campus-safety-officer-ft-pm-shift": {
      "title": "Campus Safety Officer FT PM Shift",
      "description": "Campus Safety Officer FT PM Shift | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Menu Menu and Search Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Home Human Resources Campus Safety Officer FT PM Shift Campus Safety Officer FT PM Shift Department Campus Life Overview Ursinus College is seeking full-time Campus Safety Officers (CSOs) to work an assigned schedule which may include nights, weekends, and holidays. In addition, the successful candidate will be required to work special events, attend mandatory in-service training, and staff meetings, as well as be available for overtime when necessary. Ursinus College CSOs are non-sworn, unarmed employees tasked with patrolling the 170-acre campus to discourage criminal behavior and interact with all members of the college community to promote a sense of well-being and security and enhance the quality of life on the Ursinus College campus. Ursinus College CSOs are expected to be ambassadors for the college and provide exceptional customer service to all members of the college community. Successful candidates will not only show an aptitude for handling emergencies but also demonstrate interpersonal skills, professionalism, and a team-oriented mindset to serve the college community and create a safe environment for learning and growth. Determination of shift will be based on department needs and is subject to change with reasonable notice. Responsibilities Safeguard life and property, and interdiction of criminal activity through high visibility, vehicular, bike, and foot patrol on the campus Respond to non-emergency and emergency situations, including alarms, lockouts, medical and mental health crises, and crimes in progress and summon municipal Police, Fire, and EMS assistance when required Conduct initial investigations, collect witness statements, photograph scenes, and prepare and submit concise and comprehensive incident reports Regulate campus vehicular and pedestrian traffic to enhance safety and expedite flow. Enforce college parking regulations Identify, report, and resolve conditions and circumstances that are hazardous to people and property. Inspect buildings and properties for proper maintenance and working order Secure and unlock campus buildings in accordance with schedules and procedures Administer First Aid and CPR when necessary Provide escorts and shuttle transportation to members of the campus community as assigned Monitor surveillance camera system and perform dispatcher duties as part of normal duties Maintain confidentiality of information This position is a Campus Security Authority as designated under the Jeanne Clery Act of 1990 This description is intended to describe the general level and nature of work performed by the person/people assigned to this position. It is not to be construed as an exhaustive list of duties and responsibilities of the person/people so assigned. Requirements and Qualifications Qualifications High School diploma or GED and good work history required Valid driver’s license, clean driving record, and at least one year driving experience Ability to operate a motor vehicle at night and in poor weather conditions Proven computer literacy (Microsoft Office Word, Excel) Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communications skills are required Must be capable of passing a background investigation including criminal records check Understanding of higher education setting and ability to work with persons of diverse cultures and backgrounds Physical Demands Patrolling assigned areas during all types of weather conditions Ability to sit or stand for prolonged period, must also be able to bend, stoop, squat, reach above shoulder level, kneel, lift and carry, push and pull up to 50 pounds Walk up and down stairs throughout the scheduled shift as required Specific vision abilities required by this job include close vision, distance vision, depth perception, and the ability to focus Ability to talk, hear, and walk without assistance Ability to report to work for assigned shift and perform required duties for a minimum of eight (8) hours per day up to a maximum of (16) hours per day Schedules consist of 8, 10, and 12-hour shifts Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege ursinuscollege Down goes Hopkins! 🐻🏈 @ursinus_football earns a memorable 35-14 BearFest victory to improve to 6-0 on the season! 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege The BearFest fun rolls on 🕺 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege Lots are packed, energy is high, BearFest is here 🎉 2 days ago Share End of post. Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved. Request Information Get information about Ursinus College"
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/11512-controller": {
      "title": "Controller",
      "description": "Controller | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Menu Menu and Search Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Home Human Resources Controller Controller Department Finance and Administration Overview The Opportunity : Ursinus College seeks a Controller to lead the College’s accounting operations and serve as a principal financial partner to the Vice President for Finance and Administration. This role is built for an accountant who wants both halves of the job. One half is stewardship: accurate books, a disciplined close, a clean audit, and internal controls that hold. The other half is strategy — the Controller at Ursinus is not confined to the rearview mirror. You will help shape the multi-year financial model, support the Board’s budget and long-range planning work, and ensure that the numbers underneath every major institutional decision are ones the College can defend. Ursinus is a residential liberal arts college of approximately 1,300 students and, like much of the sector, is actively managing enrollment pressure and a multi-year effort to align its cost structure with revenue. That work succeeds only if the underlying financial information is fast, clean, and trusted. Guaranteeing that is why this position exists. Responsibilities Key Responsibilities Financial Reporting and the Annual Audit: Own the integrity, accuracy, and timeliness of all accounting and financial reporting for the College. Produce monthly, quarterly, and annual financial statements, and deliver them on a schedule leadership can plan around. Manage the year-end close and serve as primary liaison to the external auditors, coordinating the annual audit from planning through issuance of the audited financial statements. Prepare and review the audit schedules, footnote support, and management representations that underpin the financial statements. Support financial reporting to the Board of Trustees and its Audit and Finance committees. Accounting Operations and Internal Control: Direct all general accounting activity, ensuring completeness and compliance with GAAP and applicable regulation. Maintain the chart of accounts and fund structure, and govern changes to both. Design, document, monitor, and continuously strengthen internal controls over financial transactions, including segregation of duties and delegation of authority. Ensure timely and accurate processing of accounts payable, disbursements, purchasing, and contract administration. Oversee reconciliation of all balance sheet accounts on a defined cycle, with exceptions escalated and cleared rather than carried forward. Endowment, Debt, Capital, and Restricted Funds: Oversee endowment accounting, including unitization, the annual spending distribution, and compliance with donor restrictions and UPMIFA. Reconcile gift and pledge activity with Advancement, and ensure restricted funds are released and reported correctly. Account for capital projects and fixed assets from authorization through capitalization and depreciation. Maintain debt accounting and monitor compliance with bond covenants; prepare continuing disclosure filings and support rating agency and lender reporting. Oversee grant accounting for federal, state, and private awards, including financial reporting to sponsors. Strategic Partnership with the VPFA: Serve as a thought partner to the VPFA on the College’s financial position, translating accounting results into implications leadership can act on. Collaborate on the accounting foundation for the multi-year financial model such as actuals, run rates, commitments, and the constraints that govern what is possible. Monitor cash position and near-term liquidity, and produce the cash forecasting that supports operating and capital decisions. Advise on the accounting and reporting consequences of proposed initiatives, new revenue ventures, and financing structures before decisions are made. Compliance and Tax: Ensure compliance with federal, state, and local requirements, including IRS, U.S. Department of Education, and funding agency regulation. Oversee preparation of Forms 990 and 990-T and related filings, and manage exposure areas such as unrelated business income and nonresident withholding. Oversee institutional financial reporting obligations, including the IPEDS finance components and other required external surveys. Monitor emerging accounting standards and regulatory changes, assess their impact on the College, and implement them on schedule. Maintain and periodically refresh the College’s financial policies. Team Leadership: Supervise, develop, and mentor the Controller’s Office staff across general accounting, accounts payable, purchasing, and contracts. Set clear standards for accuracy, turnaround, and service, and hold the team to them. Build depth and cross-coverage so that critical functions do not depend on a single person. Foster a culture of service, precision, and continuous improvement across a team that supports every department on campus. Systems and Process Improvement: In partnership with IT, own the operation, configuration, and optimization of the College’s financial systems, currently Oracle Cloud ERP. Identify and implement process improvements that increase efficiency, accuracy, and transparency, and reduce manual effort in the close. Partner with Information Technology and functional departments to ensure clean integration of financial data across systems. First-Year Priorities: Deliver a clean, on-time audit and a shortened, better-documented year-end close. Assess the current state of internal controls and reconciliation discipline, and close identified gaps. Advance the College’s use of Oracle Cloud ERP, with an emphasis on reporting and automation. Establish the recurring financial reporting package the VPFA, President, and the Board rely on. Requirements and Qualifications Required: Bachelor’s degree in accounting, finance, or a closely related field. Active CPA license. Minimum of seven years of progressively responsible accounting experience, including at least three years supervising professional staff. Strong working knowledge of GAAP as applied to not-for-profit organizations, including net asset classification, contribution and endowment accounting, and fund accounting. Demonstrated ownership of a year-end close and an external financial statement audit. Proficiency with an enterprise ERP system and advanced Excel skills. Exceptional organizational skills, attention to detail, and a record of meeting deadlines. Preferred Skills and Competencies Preferred: Experience in higher education or another complex not-for-profit organization. Master’s degree in accounting, finance, or business administration. Hands-on experience with Oracle Cloud ERP, or leadership of an ERP implementation or optimization. Depth in endowment accounting, UPMIFA, gift and grant accounting, and tax-exempt debt. Familiarity with federal compliance requirements, including Uniform Guidance and the Single Audit. Experience presenting financial information to a governing board or its committees. Competencies and Attributes: Strategic and analytical. Sees past the transaction to the pattern, and past the pattern to the decision. Clear communicator. Explains technical accounting to non-accountants such as deans, department chairs, trustees, without condescension and without losing precision. Collaborative leader. Builds a team people want to join and want to stay on. Unimpeachable integrity. High ethical standards and absolute discretion with confidential information. Service-oriented. Treats colleagues across campus as constituents to be served, not requesters to be processed. Steady under pressure. Keeps quality high through close, audit, and budget season alike. Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege ursinuscollege Down goes Hopkins! 🐻🏈 @ursinus_football earns a memorable 35-14 BearFest victory to improve to 6-0 on the season! 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege The BearFest fun rolls on 🕺 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege Lots are packed, energy is high, BearFest is here 🎉 2 days ago Share End of post. Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved. Request Information Get information about Ursinus College"
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/11564-director-of-camps-and-conferences": {
      "title": "Director of Camps and Conferences",
      "description": "Director of Camps and Conferences | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Menu Menu and Search Home Human Resources Director of Camps and Conferences Director of Camps and Conferences Department Advancement Overview Reporting to the Associate Vice President for Advancement, the Director of Camps and Conferences leads the College’s camps, conferences, external facility rentals, and related programs while developing new mission-aligned opportunities that extend the resources, facilities, and educational expertise of Ursinus College to broader audiences and generate additional revenue and other resources in support of the College. The Director manages a repeatable framework through which new opportunities are identified, researched, assessed, modeled, piloted, evaluated, and, where successful, scaled. Working collaboratively across the College, the Director convenes and leads teams that leverage faculty, staff, and institutional expertise to assess market demand, financial and operational feasibility, mission alignment, potential for earned revenue and external support, and opportunities for student engagement. The Director oversees a developing portfolio that may include non-credit-bearing lifelong and short-form learning, workforce and professional development, youth and pre-college programming, and other programs and services beyond the traditional undergraduate curriculum under the umbrella of the Ursinus Academy. Responsibilities Camps, Conferences & Facility Rentals Lead the management and growth of the College’s external camps, conferences, meetings, retreats, facility rentals, and other appropriate programs that utilize campus assets. Manage the College’s event and facility rental program, establishing efficient and customer-focused approaches to inquiries, pricing, scheduling, contracting, coordination, and service delivery. Build awareness of Ursinus as a destination for camps, conferences, meetings, events, and other external uses and develop new and repeat business. Partner with appropriate campus departments to ensure effective program delivery, risk management, and stewardship of College resources. Identify and leverage technology, third-party platforms, and other solutions that streamline administration, improve the client experience, and support growth. Monitor utilization, client experience, financial performance, and other measures and recommend opportunities for improvement and expansion. Program Development & Portfolio Strategy Develop and manage a repeatable framework for identifying, researching, assessing, modeling, pressure-testing, piloting, and evaluating mission-aligned programs and services with the potential to generate additional revenue or resources for the College. Identify opportunities to extend College expertise, facilities, programs, and resources to broader audiences in ways that advance the College’s mission and financial sustainability. Assess opportunities for earned revenue as well as philanthropic, government, grant, sponsorship, or other external support, partnering with appropriate colleagues to evaluate and pursue potential funding opportunities. Establish consistent criteria for determining whether concepts should be piloted, refined, expanded, replicated, or discontinued. Maintain a pipeline of emerging opportunities and recommend priorities for development and investment. Partner with Marketing & Communications and other campus colleagues to support promotion and audience development. Lifelong Learning Work closely with the Provost’s Office to lead the business development of the Ursinus Academy as a platform for non-credit-bearing educational experiences beyond the traditional undergraduate curriculum and across stages of life. Partner with faculty and other subject-matter experts to translate College expertise into educational offerings for broader audiences, with faculty and experts providing instruction and educational content. Explore and develop opportunities in lifelong and enrichment learning, workforce and professional development, training and development, youth and pre-college learning, and other short-form educational experiences. Work with faculty and campus partners to determine appropriate audiences, formats, delivery approaches, and financial or funding models. Evaluate programs based on participation, quality, mission alignment, audience response, and financial sustainability. Financial Management & Assessment Work closely with the Director of Auxiliary Revenue to develop and manage budgets, revenue projections, pricing approaches, and financial goals for programs within the portfolio ensuring a positive return on investment. Monitor participation, utilization, revenue, expenses, contribution margin, and other measures of program performance. Requirements and Qualifications Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree required. Minimum of 3 years of progressively responsible experience in conferences and events, hospitality, sales, advancement, or related field. Demonstrated experience implementing new programs or services, including responsibility for budgets, revenue, or other measurable outcomes. Strong planning, analytical, project management, and problem-solving skills, with the ability to lead complex, cross-functional initiatives from concept through implementation and evaluation. Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with faculty, staff, senior leaders, external partners, and clients. Proficiency with Microsoft Office applications and ability to learn and effectively use technology platforms relevant to the position. Commitment to professionalism, collaboration, sound judgment, and responsible stewardship of College resources. Work Environment: Ability to sit for extended periods while performing computer-based work. Ability to occasionally lift and transport materials weighing up to 25 pounds. Ability to move throughout campus and College facilities in support of programs and events. Ability to work evenings and weekends. Ability to travel for meetings, program development, and other College business as needed. Departmental Accountabilities: Develops strong working relationships with faculty and academic and administrative partners whose expertise, facilities, services, and programs may support new initiatives. Coordinates with colleagues responsible for fundraising, grant development, external partnerships, APEX, career development, enrollment, alumni engagement, and other College priorities to connect programs, funding opportunities, and relationships appropriately. Provides support to office leadership, as needed, for priority projects. Possesses knowledge and experience with technology and systems that support program development, financial administration, and College operations. Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/11548-director-of-student-accounts": {
      "title": "Director of Student Accounts",
      "description": "Director of Student Accounts | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Menu Menu and Search Home Human Resources Director of Student Accounts Director of Student Accounts Department Enrollment Management Overview The Director of Student Accounts leads a department which plays a critical role in providing leadership in enrollment management, reporting directly to the Vice President for Enrollment Management. The primary role of the Director of Student Accounts is to maintain the accuracy of student tuition/fee accounts and oversee the student billing process to ensure timely and accurate billing statements. The Director of Student Accounts is responsible for reviewing tuition and fees and approving both account adjustments and accurate student refunds. In addition, the Director of Student Accounts will act as cashier for all operational receipts and disbursement of all financial aid funds. As a result, the Director of Student Accounts will be the expert institutional resource for all student account related policies and procedures as well as compliance with internal and external policies and guidelines. The Director of Student Accounts will collaborate with other areas to implement and improve processes and procedures that increase service to students and strengthen administrative operations. Responsibilities Maintains the accuracy of student tuition and fee accounts and oversees the billing and receipt process to ensure timely and accurate billing statements for students. Responsible for all incoming, current, and past students’ accounts. Ensures that all College funds, including student payments, returned payments, receipts from other financial institutions and other sources are properly accounted for and deposited into the prescribed College bank account in a timely manner. Reconciles all General Ledger accounts assigned to the Student Accounts Office. Maintains policies and procedures related to Student Accounts’ Office and receivables. Supervise and lead the staff by providing excellent management, training, and support. Ensures office coverage and excellent customer service. Counsels and advises students and families regarding the tuition and fee payment process and options available to discharge the obligation including but not limited to payment plans, loans, and personal payment arrangements with the College. Regularly interacts with student and families, including and dispute resolution. Requires strong customer service and conflict resolution skills Remains informed of updates/patches regarding student accounts system. Troubleshoots and resolves issues related to the student side of the system. Proactively build and test tuition and fee assessment for each semester. Coordinates with Information Technology as it relates to reporting, PCI Compliance, 1098T tax reporting, and third-party vendor integration. Processes 1098T statements in SIS and uploads to IRS on an annual basis Conducts and complies with internal and external audit requirements and coordinates for a successful annual fiscal close and audit in coordination with the office of Finance Maintains excellent control of departmental budget, consistently looking for ways to reduce costs without sacrificing service. Maintains student financial services information on the department website in coordination with Marketing and Communications. Oversees collections with outside collection agencies Participates in student check-in and admissions events Oversees tuition insurance program and claim processing Oversees adjustments to student accounts (leave, W/D, dismissal, graduation, and miscellaneous departmental charges) Oversees student deposit process Oversees student health insurance waiver process Monitors Student Accounts email address Other duties as assigned Requirements and Qualifications Required: Bachelor’s degree Required: Minimum of 3-5 years student accounts experience in a higher education setting, with preference for demonstrated additional responsibilities over time Candidate must possess thorough knowledge of Title IV regulations and have a commitment to financial aid to help enroll and retain a diverse student body Expertise with an SIS/CRM system is essential to the position. Experience in data analysis and reporting, including mastery of Excel A collaborative and transparent approach to problem-solving A desire to join and foster a dynamic, transformative campus culture Demonstrates commitment to diversity Excellent quantitative analytic skills, writing and communication skills, demonstrated organization and leadership skills. Ability to perform with a high degree of diplomacy, confidentiality, and professionalism. Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/9046-director-of-disability-and-access": {
      "title": "Director, Office of Disability and Access",
      "description": "Director, Office of Disability and Access | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Menu Menu and Search Home Human Resources Director, Office of Disability and Access Director, Office of Disability and Access Department Institute for Student Success Overview Job Description Reporting to: Senior Director, Institute for Student Success / Chief Retention Officer Position Summary The Director of Disability and Access provides strategic leadership for the College’s Disability and Access Office and advances the institution’s commitment to creating an accessible, inclusive, and supportive learning environment for students with disabilities. The Director is responsible for the overall leadership, administration, staffing, and strategic direction of the office, including the development and evaluation of programs and services that promote access, engagement, persistence, and student success. The Director supervises and develops office staff, establishes operational priorities and processes, collaborates with campus partners to address systemic barriers to access, and serves as a senior institutional resource on disability, accessibility, and neurodiversity. A significant responsibility of the position is the development and implementation of EDGE, an academic support and career readiness program, designed to support the successful transition of incoming neurodivergent students to college. The Director will build this program in collaboration with campus partners and establish a sustainable model that strengthens students’ academic preparedness, self-advocacy, executive functioning, connection to campus resources, and sense of belonging. Responsibilities Leadership + Oversight of Disability and Access Provide vision, leadership, and strategic direction for the Disability and Access Office. Oversee the overall administration and daily operations of the office, establishing priorities, procedures, service expectations, and workflows that promote consistent and equitable support for students. Supervise, evaluate, mentor, and support professional, administrative, and student staff within the office. Establish annual goals and priorities for the office that align with institutional student success, retention, belonging, and accessibility goals. Manage the office budget and identify opportunities to expand resources and programming through institutional funding, grants, partnerships, and other sources. Develop and maintain systems for tracking office activity, student engagement, accommodation trends, and program outcomes. Ensure continuity and quality of services during periods of staff transition, high-volume demand, and other operational challenges. Lead regular review and improvement of office policies, procedures, technology, and service delivery models. E.D.G.E: Academic Support + Career Readiness Program Lead the design, development, implementation, and ongoing assessment of the E.D.G.E program for incoming students. Develop a program model that supports the transition from high school to college and addresses common areas of transition, including executive functioning, time management, self-advocacy, academic planning, navigating campus resources, communication, and social connection. Establish program learning outcomes, curriculum, eligibility criteria, participation processes, staffing models, and assessment measures. Collaborate with Admissions, Student Success and Advising, Academic Support, Counseling and Wellness, Residential Life, Career Development, faculty, and other campus partners to create an integrated transition experience. Develop opportunities for participating students to build relationships with peers, faculty, staff, and upperclass student mentors. Incorporate experiential activities that familiarize students with academic expectations, campus systems, technology, support resources, and community-building opportunities. Develop and oversee a sustainable staffing and funding model for the program. Evaluate program outcomes, student experiences, and persistence data to identify opportunities for continuous improvement and expansion. Explore opportunities to extend the program’s impact beyond the summer through first-year programming, peer mentoring, workshops, and ongoing community-building opportunities. Campus Collaboration + Institutional Leadership Serve as a campus leader and subject-matter expert on disability access, inclusion, and neurodiversity. Provide consultation and training to faculty and staff regarding accommodations, universal design, accessibility, neurodiversity, and inclusive practices. Partner with campus offices to identify and address systemic barriers that may impede student access, engagement, or success. Participate in relevant student success, retention, accessibility, and campus care committees and initiatives. Build strong relationships with students and student organizations and incorporate student perspectives into program and policy development. Promote a campus culture that recognizes disability and neurodiversity as important dimensions of student identity and campus diversity. Collaborate with Information Technology, Facilities, Communications, and other operational areas to advance physical, digital, and instructional accessibility. Program Development, Assessment, + Strategic Planning Develop and implement initiatives that promote the retention, persistence, engagement, and success of students with disabilities. Use institutional and office-level data to identify trends, evaluate student needs, and inform strategic priorities. Establish measurable outcomes for Disability and Access programs and services and communicate results to institutional leadership. Conduct regular reviews of office practices and programs to identify opportunities for greater efficiency, accessibility, and student impact. Research emerging practices and national trends in disability services, neurodiversity, and inclusive higher education. Identify opportunities to expand or redesign services in response to changing student needs. Prepare reports, presentations, proposals, and recommendations for College leadership as needed. Requirements and Qualifications Qualifications: Master’s degree in higher education, student affairs, disability studies, counseling, social work, education, or a related field. Progressive professional experience in disability services, accessibility, student affairs, or a related higher education environment. Demonstrated experience supervising and developing professional staff. Knowledge of disability access requirements and best practices in higher education. Demonstrated ability to develop programs and collaborate effectively across departments. Strong organizational, communication, problem-solving, and interpersonal skills. Commitment to student-centered practice, accessibility, inclusion, and student success. Preferred Skills and Competencies Preferred: Experience leading a college or university disability/accessibility office or comparable student support unit. Experience developing transition, bridge, summer, or first-year programs. Experience with assessment, retention initiatives, and student success data. Experience developing staff training and campus-wide professional development. Familiarity with universal design for learning and universal design principles. Experience working with student information systems, case management systems, or disability/accessibility management platforms Application Interested candidates should apply here . Once you create an applicant account, you will be prompted to upload your cover letter, resume, and a list with contact information for three references to the applicant database. Ursinus is an EEO employer. Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/11394-internship-coordinator": {
      "title": "Internship Coordinator",
      "description": "Internship Coordinator | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Menu Menu and Search Home Human Resources Internship Coordinator Internship Coordinator Department Academic Affairs Overview The Internship Coordinator supports the College’s mission of fostering student growth through experiential learning opportunities that connect academic study with career exploration and professional development. This position is responsible for developing internship partnerships, advising students throughout the internship process, and collaborating with faculty and campus partners to expand access to high-impact learning experiences. The Internship Coordinator also leads assessment efforts related to experiential learning, utilizing data and stakeholder feedback to elevate student learning, program effectiveness, and career readiness outcomes. Working within a student-centered liberal arts environment, the Internship Coordinator helps students translate classroom learning into meaningful professional experiences while supporting institutional goals related to retention, career readiness, and postgraduate outcomes. Responsibilities Specific Responsibilities: Student Advising + Support Advise students individually on internship exploration, applications, professional communication, resumes, and interview preparation Support students in identifying internships aligned with their academic interests, career goals, and personal strengths Guide students through internship registration, approval, and reflection processes Assist students in navigating workplace challenges and professional expectations during internship experiences Develop and deliver workshops on internship readiness, networking, career exploration, and professional skills Employer + Community Partnership Development Cultivate relationships with regional employers, nonprofit organizations, alumni, and community partners to expand internship opportunities Conduct outreach to develop new internship sites aligned with liberal arts disciplines and student interests Coordinate employer engagement events including networking programs, career panels, and internship fairs Serve as a liaison between employers, faculty, and students throughout the internship experience Program Coordination + Administration Manage internship processes, records, assessment, and reporting Coordinate internship documentation including learning agreements, evaluations, and site approvals Track student participation and internship outcomes to support institutional assessment and accreditation efforts Maintain internship and employer data within career management systems such as Handshake or student success platforms Collaborate with academic departments to support credit-bearing internships and experiential learning initiatives Campus Collaboration Partner with faculty, academic advisors, career services staff, and student success professionals to promote experiential learning opportunities Contribute to initiatives related to retention, student engagement, and career readiness Support programming for students seeking equitable access to internships Participate in orientation, admissions, and campus-wide events as needed Assessment + Program Evaluation Develop and implement assessment strategies to evaluate internship quality, student learning outcomes, and program effectiveness Collect and analyze student, employer, and faculty feedback through evaluations, surveys, and reflection activities Track internship participation, career readiness outcomes, and postgraduate success data to support institutional assessment efforts Prepare reports and presentations on experiential learning outcomes, employer engagement, and program impact for campus stakeholders and accreditation purposes Use assessment data to recommend and implement improvements to internship programming, student support, and employer partnerships Requirements and Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in higher education, counseling, student affairs, communications, business, or related field 1–3 years of relevant experience in higher education, career services, student support, employer relations, or experiential learning Demonstrated commitment to student development and inclusive student support practices Strong interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills Ability to build collaborative relationships with students, faculty, staff, alumni, and employers Experience managing multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment Preferred Skills and Competencies Master’s degree in higher education, student affairs, counseling, or related field Experience working in a small college or liberal arts environment Familiarity with experiential learning, internship coordination, or career development programming Experience with career management or student success software platforms Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/11540-systems-management-librarian": {
      "title": "Systems Management Librarian",
      "description": "Systems Management Librarian | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Menu Menu and Search Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Home Human Resources Systems Management Librarian Systems Management Librarian Department Academic Affairs Overview Summary and Scope of Position: The Systems Management Librarian is responsible for Myrin Library’s electronic resource management and operates its interlibrary loan and resource sharing service. This is a hands-on role requiring a holistic understanding of all library systems — including the library services platform, discovery layer, proxy/authentication, research guides, and resource sharing tools — and the ability to make them work together reliably day to day. The position administers and troubleshoots systems, maintains the library’s online presence, and runs ILL end to end. It manages licenses and access rights for e-books, online journals and databases. It also serves as Sciences liaison to academic departments and APEX hubs such as the Parlee Center for Science and the Common Good, providing data, statistical, and geospatial support (including ArcGIS). Strategic direction rests with the Director; this position supplies the technical execution, operational judgment, and data that inform those decisions. Governing faculty status includes participation in Faculty Meetings and eligibility to serve on faculty committees. Work Environment: Normal office conditions, with a heavy amount of detail-intensive computer work. Resource sharing and technical services responsibilities involve regular handling of physical materials, including retrieving and shelving items, packing and unpacking shipments, moving loaded book trucks, and lifting or carrying packages of up to 40 pounds. Occasional travel for professional development opportunities. Responsibilities Specific Responsibilities: Systems and Online Presence Administer and maintain library systems: OCLC WMS, WorldCat Discovery, Tipasa, LibGuides, EZproxy, ArcGIS, StackMap, EZBorrow, and others as adopted. Manage upgrades, configurations, and integrations with campus systems (SSO, SIS, LMS). Troubleshoot technical issues for students, faculty, and staff; coordinate with IT and vendors through resolution. Manage licenses, support agreements, and vendor relationships for systems and continuing electronic resources. Maintain the library’s website and Library/IT knowledge base; update content, navigation, and usability in coordination with the Director. Ensure library content meets accessibility standards, working with the Office of Disability & Access as needed. Monitor trends and emerging technologies; recommend future initiatives to the Director. Generate reports and statistics for collection management, resource sharing, and assessment (including ACRL and MISO surveys). Maintain documentation sufficient for continuity of operations. Collections and Interlibrary Loan Execute collection decisions set by the Director; flag gaps, cost changes, and renewals requiring attention; provide usage data and analysis to support collection decisions. Process ILL borrowing and lending requests end to end (Tipasa, EZBorrow): placement, sourcing, receiving, shipping, renewals, invoicing, and problem resolution. Serve as primary contact for patrons and partner/consortial libraries; ensure copyright and licensing compliance; maintain consortial agreements, policies, and turnaround standards. Streamline ILL workflows for sustainability; track and report volume and turnaround statistics; coordinate with the Director on purchase-on-demand decisions. Train, schedule, and direct student assistants across ILL; maintain procedural documentation. Teaching and Liaison Work Serve as Sciences liaison: provide instruction, research help, and research guides within the Digital Fluency program. Partner with the Parlee Center for Science and the Common Good, supporting its Fellows Program, consultation projects, and science communication work. Support APEX project teams with research strategy, source identification, and data discovery; build project- and challenge-based research guides alongside traditional department guides. Provide data and geospatial support, including ArcGIS, for APEX policy labs and student/faculty research in sustainability, public health, and civic and global leadership. Contribute workshops to APEX Experiential Learning Week and hub/center programming. Other Duties: The above statements are intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by the individual(s) assigned to this position. They are not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of responsibilities, duties, and skills required of personnel so classified. Job responsibilities and activities may be modified or changed at any time with or without notice. Job descriptions will be audited periodically by the Ursinus Human Resources department to ensure they are up-to-date and reflect the essential responsibilities and duties of the job. Requirements and Qualifications ALA-accredited MLS/MLIS. Experience administering an ILS or library services platform, particularly OCLC WMS and WorldCat Discovery. Experience with ILL/resource sharing, technical services and library instruction. Experience maintaining content in LibGuides, a CMS, or comparable platform. Detail-oriented, with strong analytical and problem-solving skills. Able to support applied, interdisciplinary research across project teams and subject areas. Flexible, self-directed, and responsive to changing priorities. Excellent written and oral communication skills and a strong service orientation. Preferred Skills and Competencies Experience with Tipasa, ILLiad, or comparable ILL platforms and consortial borrowing (e.g., EZBorrow). Experience managing electronic resources, including proxy configuration and access troubleshooting. Experience with MARC editing, batch metadata, vendor APIs, or workflow automation/scripting. Experience with GIS, particularly ArcGIS, and data-intensive research support. Experience supporting undergraduate research, experiential learning, or interdisciplinary programs. Background in an academic library or merged library/IT organization. Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege ursinuscollege Down goes Hopkins! 🐻🏈 @ursinus_football earns a memorable 35-14 BearFest victory to improve to 6-0 on the season! 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege The BearFest fun rolls on 🕺 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege Lots are packed, energy is high, BearFest is here 🎉 2 days ago Share End of post. Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved. Request Information Get information about Ursinus College"
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/11515-head-coach-womens-flag-football": {
      "title": "Head Coach Women’s Flag Football",
      "description": "Head Coach Women’s Flag Football | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Menu Menu and Search Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Home Human Resources Head Coach Women’s Flag Football Head Coach Women’s Flag Football Department Athletics Overview Summary and Scope of Position: The Head Women’s Flag Football Coach is responsible for the administration and organization of a successful women’s flag football program. Responsibilities Specific Responsibilities: Prepare, schedule and organize daily practice sessions, travel schedules, game preparation for competition, and off-season training; devise team rules and expectations Organize and administer the recruiting of prospective student-athletes in accordance with the NCAA and Ursinus College policies and procedures; Hire, organize, direct, and mentor the coaching staff to carry out the goals and objectives of the program; support all athletes, coaches and staff on and off the field Provide general advising, mentoring and support to student-athletes and work to ensure their academic progress and ongoing eligibility, keeping the College’s mission and values in mind; Responsibly manage allocated budget; Coordinate fundraising activities and alumni engagement through the Office of Advancement related to your sport; Engage in regular consultation with athletic training personnel to implement appropriate risk management strategies and maximize the safety and overall wellness of student-athletes; Work collaboratively with the Compliance Officer in regards to adhering to all rules and regulations of the NCAA; Field a team that will be as competitive as possible within the guidelines set by the NCAA and Ursinus College; Engage in ethical decision-making and outstanding sportsmanship; be mindful of representing Athletics in a professional/positive way Requirements and Qualifications Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree required, Master’s degree preferred Knowledge of rules and techniques of the sport Excellent verbal and written communication skills Excellent organizational, delegation and planning ability Knowledge of NCAA Division III rules and regulations Experience with recruiting and fundraising Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege ursinuscollege Down goes Hopkins! 🐻🏈 @ursinus_football earns a memorable 35-14 BearFest victory to improve to 6-0 on the season! 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege The BearFest fun rolls on 🕺 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege Lots are packed, energy is high, BearFest is here 🎉 2 days ago Share End of post. Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved. Request Information Get information about Ursinus College"
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/11538-head-coach-womens-softball": {
      "title": "Head Coach Women’s Softball",
      "description": "Head Coach Women’s Softball | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Menu Menu and Search Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Home Human Resources Head Coach Women’s Softball Head Coach Women’s Softball Department Athletics Overview Summary and Scope of Position: The Head Coach is responsible for the administration and organization of a successful athletic program that competes in the Centennial Conference at the NCAA Division III level. Responsibilities Specific Responsibilities: Prepare, schedule and organize daily practice sessions, travel schedules, game preparation for competition, and off-season training; devise team rules and expectations Organize and administer the recruiting of prospective student-athletes in accordance with the NCAA, Centennial Conference, and Ursinus College policies and procedures Hire, organize, direct, and mentor the coaching staff to carry out the goals and objectives of the program; support all athletes, coaches and staff on and off the field Provide general advising, mentoring and support to student-athletes and work to ensure their academic progress and ongoing eligibility, keeping the College’s mission and values in mind Responsibly manage the allocated budget Coordinate fundraising activities and alumni engagement through the Office of Advancement related to your sport Engage in regular consultation with athletic training personnel to implement appropriate risk management strategies and maximize the safety and overall wellness of student-athletes Work collaboratively with the Compliance Officer in regards to adhering to all rules and regulations of the NCAA Field a team that will be as competitive as possible within the guidelines set by the NCAA, CC and Ursinus College Engage in ethical decision-making and outstanding sportsmanship; be mindful of representing Athletics in a professional/positive way Additional sport specific duties related to student-athlete and facility safety Execute all these responsibilities as head coach in a manner that reflects an ongoing commitment to the excellence, integrity and success of the softball program, the Department of Athletics, and Ursinus College. Other Duties: The above statements are intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by the individual(s) assigned to this position. They are not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of responsibilities, duties, and skills required of personnel so classified. Job responsibilities and activities may be modified or changed at any time with or without notice. Job descriptions will be audited periodically by the Ursinus Human Resources department to ensure they are up-to-date and reflect the essential responsibilities and duties of the job. Requirements and Qualifications Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree required, Master’s degree preferred Knowledge of rules and techniques of the sport Excellent verbal and written communication skills Excellent organizational, delegation and planning ability Knowledge of NCAA Division III and Centennial Conference rules and regulations Experience with recruiting and fundraising Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege ursinuscollege Down goes Hopkins! 🐻🏈 @ursinus_football earns a memorable 35-14 BearFest victory to improve to 6-0 on the season! 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege The BearFest fun rolls on 🕺 2 days ago Share End of post. ursinuscollege Lots are packed, energy is high, BearFest is here 🎉 2 days ago Share End of post. Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved. Request Information Get information about Ursinus College"
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/10973-lecturer-academic-affairs": {
      "title": "Lecturer",
      "description": "Lecturer - Academic Affairs | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Menu Menu and Search Home Human Resources Lecturer - Academic Affairs Lecturer - Academic Affairs Department Office of Academic Affairs Overview Ursinus College is inviting applications for multiple adjunct teaching positions in a variety of disciplines for Spring 2026. The successful candidates will have demonstrated the potential for excellence in related professional experience and past teaching roles, specifically in the liberal arts setting. The selected candidates will have the opportunity to teach 1-2 courses beginning January 26, 2026 and ending May 20, 2026. Should your expertise, knowledge, skills, and abilities be an optimal match for one of our available courses, the Provost’s Office will be reaching out by January 12, 2026. Ursinus is an EEO employer. Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://www.ursinus.edu/live/profiles/11503-apex-fellow-corporate-relations-athletic": {
      "title": "APEX Fellow",
      "description": "APEX Fellow - Corporate Relations & Athletic Partnerships | Human Resources | Ursinus College Skip to main content Visit Apply Request Info Make a Gift Search Ursinus.edu Menu Menu and Search Close Expanded Main Menu Search Ursinus.edu Search About Academics Admission APEX Life at Ursinus News & Events Careers & Outcomes Athletics Graduate Partnerships Arts at Ursinus Give to Ursinus Request Info Visit Apply Information for: Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents Admitted Students Menu Menu and Search Home Human Resources APEX Fellow - Corporate Relations & Athletic Partnerships APEX Fellow - Corporate Relations & Athletic Partnerships Department Advancement Overview As Ursinus continues to advance APEX and applied liberal arts education, we wish to extend those principles beyond graduation while supporting the College’s evolving operating model. This Post-Baccalaureate APEX Fellows Program creates practical, project-based early career professional experience for recent graduates while expanding the College’s capacity to execute strategic initiatives. Each Fellow will be assigned a primary area of focus that provides entry-level, real-world experience within a professional functional area, complemented by secondary work and learning experiences that broaden their understanding of higher education, institutional operations, and cross-functional collaboration. The Corporate Relations Fellow – Athletics Partnerships will develop and execute the “Fill the Fence” Corporate Sponsorship Campaign, securing local and regional business partners to generate budget-relieving support for the Bears Athletics Club while establishing scalable corporate sponsorship processes that can support future athletics and institutional initiatives. What You’ll Gain: Applied: Direct engagement with employer partners, sponsorship cultivation, and partnership development strategy. Professional: Proficiency with industry-standard CRM tools (e.g., Raiser’s Edge NXT, VanillaSoft) APEX Fellow Descriptions.docx and an understanding of how institutions build and sustain external relationships. Experiential: Real-world exposure to negotiation, proposal development, and cross-functional collaboration between academic and administrative offices. This fellowship will position you for careers in business development, corporate relations, strategic partnerships, and higher education advancement. Responsibilities Primary Responsibilities: Research, identify, and prioritize local and regional businesses and vendors as prospective corporate sponsors. Conduct coordinated virtual and in-person outreach and help connect prospective partners with sponsorship opportunities that align with their business goals. Create and maintain sponsorship materials, outreach templates, checklists, and processes under the guidance of Advancement staff. Coordinate with Advancement and Athletics staff to support the activation and fulfillment of sponsor benefits, including signage, game-day announcements, and digital recognition. Maintain accurate sponsor and prospect records and track outreach, commitments, benefit fulfillment, and other program results using Raiser’s Edge NXT and VanillaSoft. Support sponsor stewardship and renewal efforts through personalized outreach, impact updates, and year-end recaps, and refine processes and materials based on results and staff guidance. Secondary: Support other office, administrative, and event-based Corporate Relations, Athletics fundraising, and Advancement initiatives as assigned to gain broader exposure to the work. Application Interested candidates should apply here Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Ursinus College is a selective, independent, co-educational, residential liberal arts college of approximately 1500 students located about 25 miles northwest of center city Philadelphia. With a diverse community of students, it is an expectation of all faculty to contribute to the inclusion, engagement, and success of all students. Ursinus is an EEO employer.  Ursinus College does not discriminate on grounds of race, color, national origin, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, age, creed, ancestry, veteran status, marital status, disability, or other classification protected by applicable law in the administration of any of its educational programs or activities or with respect to employment. E-Verify: Ursinus College participates in the federal E-Verify program to confirm the identity and employment authorization of all newly hired employees. Employment is contingent upon completion of the Form I-9 and verification through E-Verify. This position may be offered at a different title or level, depending on the background, qualifications, and experience of the candidate selected. @ursinuscollege Powered by Curator.io Campus Map Webmail Library Editor Login Offices and Services Employment Contact Us Berman Museum Title IX and Sex Discrimination Campus Store & Books Web Accessibility Calendars Operating Hours and Service Updates Facebook X / Twitter Instagram YouTube ©\n            2026 Ursinus College. All rights reserved."
    }
